 <a id="use-this-package"></a>
 ## Use this package
 
+`session/aside` creates a read-only Session for each quick question using the model selected in the main conversation. Each request reads a bounded progress summary from the parent. Quick answers default to 512 output tokens and use the General Settings reasoning effort when the model supports it.
+
+`session/aside` with `kind: 'side'` opens one persistent discussion per main conversation. Opening it again returns the most recent generation, including side conversations saved by released desktop versions. Earlier generations are readable history. Each follow-up reads completed turns from the main conversation, up to `sideContextBytes` (65,536 bytes by default). The main conversation can call `read_side_context` to read its current side discussion within the same byte limit. The result is logged in the main conversation. Reading sends no message to the side. `session/resetAside` accepts the exact side identity. A manual clear creates a new generation. Closing a tab clears it only when `sideClearOnClose` is enabled (default off). A retry against a retired generation returns the current identity. The new generation keeps the side’s model and permissions. Other clients receive `api-session/side-reset` for the parent.
+
+The session/predict endpoint requests a suffix for an unsent draft from the current Session model. The Session records the exact outgoing message, route, request ID and outcome. Accepted suggestions remain editable. Prediction records written by released desktop versions remain readable.
+
+The request contains `sessionId`, the unsent `draft`, a nonnegative integer `revision` and an optional `locale` for language-neutral drafts. The reply echoes the revision with its `suggestion`. An empty suffix clears the preview. The client discards late revisions and cancels requests when the draft changes.
+
+When a released DSHU v4 Session contains composer suggestions, reopening it preserves the recorded draft, suggestion, and any provider usage. A suggestion appears as a user message only if the user submitted it.
+
 Fork keeps the inclusive `atSeq` request field and the default completed-turn cut. The command passes the observed history as the fork source: the stored parent prefix through `persistedEventCount`, then the child's marker and cold-read repair events. A live parent's log replaces that prefix in the factory, and a sharing backend drains the parent writer before referencing it; a parent whose stored history is shorter than the cut creates no child. See the [fork upgrade guide](../../../docs/upgrade-guide/v0.2.2-rc.3/session-fork-source/guide.md) for the creation parameters.
 
 Prompt retries use the request identity in the first committed inbox insertion, including after the message is claimed or removed. Admission checks again after asynchronous attachment preparation, so concurrent retries accept one input. Acceptance acknowledges insertion, not execution or completion.
