+++ b/packages/client/connection/README.md
@@ -61,6 +61,8 @@ Authenticated shared HTTP requests pass through the `connection/request` waterfa
 
 An ended `$events` stream, a Remote stream error, a non-ready opening item, or a malformed event item invalidates the current generation. A pending handshake logs a slow-Host warning after 3 seconds and logs the readiness timeout and aborts after 15 seconds by default, including time spent waiting for the physical socket. The source must stop delivery, release resources, and settle after cancellation before a replacement starts; late readiness from a cancelled source cannot publish a generation. While the browser reports network availability, the controller publishes `connecting` and retries with 50%–100% jitter under caps of 500ms, 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, and 10s, continuing at the final cap until recovery. Every retry asks Gateway to replace the physical WebSocket once and reopens `$events`.
 
+Desktop and worker transports that own a local Host keep their connection when the operating system goes offline. A remote web client still suspends retries until the network returns.
+
 `ctx.connection.reconnect()` interrupts active work, resets the sequence, and starts retry 1 immediately. Browser `offline` aborts active work, publishes `disconnected`, and suspends automatic attempts; the next `online` transition resets the sequence and starts at the 500ms tier. Only a ready item publishes `connected`. Gateway mux owns no independent retry schedule.
 
 Set the Host Connection row's `config.recovery` to override retry caps, the growth factor, or handshake warning and cancellation times; the [configuration catalog](../../../docs/config-catalog.md#deepseek-aidsh-client-connection) lists accepted fields. `recovery` reaches the browser through the plugin's [synced Config](../modules/README.md#synced-plugin-configuration). The Host validates the values when the plugin loads. The Client reads them once when its plugin activates, validates them before providing Connection, and uses them as defaults when Gateway starts its loop; a page that receives no value uses the built-in timing above, and explicit `start()` timing overrides take precedence. The growth factor must be finite and at least one. Readiness, failure, cancellation, or a hard deadline that occurs before the warning cancels that warning. A change takes effect on the next page load or plugin reactivation.
